/*********************************************************************
Configuration and initialization file of the PostgreSQL <Project>
database. The database holds structured information that is logically
grouped by the domain of usage, one schema per domain. The file builds
the database from scratch, and a pipeline can run it again to bring
an existing database to the latest version.

All the DDL statements are guarded, i.e., the initialization scripts
should not fail if an object exists: schemas, tables and indexes use
IF NOT EXISTS, functions, views and triggers use OR REPLACE, and the
types and domains are created in a DO block that checks the catalog.

The whole run is wrapped in a single transaction, so a failure
anywhere rolls the database back rather than leaving a half-built
schema. Run it from this directory against an existing database:

    psql -d <database> -f initialize.sql

Extensions, publications and subscriptions are not created here: run
the scripts of ``extensions/``, ``publications/`` and
``subscriptions/`` separately, after this file; a subscription cannot
run inside a transaction.

Some seed data may contain accented values; so the encoding is set
explicitly as "UTF-8" rather than inherited from the console. The
rules target PostgreSQL 18 and later.

Copyright © [<year>] <copyright-holder>
*********************************************************************/

\set ON_ERROR_STOP ON

SELECT TO_CHAR(
    CLOCK_TIMESTAMP(), 'YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS.MS TZ'
) AS ts \gset

\echo 'Starting <Project> Initialization' :ts

/*********************************************************************
Schemas by Domain of Usage: the DDL of every schema lives in its own
directory under ``schemas/`` with its own ``initialize.sql``, and is
loaded in a strict order, as the later schemas reference the earlier
ones:

    1. ``core``     : shared master data and helper functions that
        every other schema references; always initialized first.
    2. ``auth``     : identity - users, clients, roles and sessions.
    3. ``events``   : what happened in the system - transactions,
        bills, jobs and logs; references ``core`` and ``auth``.
    4. ``projects`` : objects of a single project or product.

NOTE: Never create application objects in the ``public`` schema on a
production server. It is on the default ``search_path`` of every
role, and before PostgreSQL 15 every role could create objects in it
(CVE-2018-1058); a database upgraded from an older release still
needs ``REVOKE CREATE ON SCHEMA public FROM PUBLIC``.
*********************************************************************/

BEGIN;

\echo '  >> Creating Core Schema ...'
\encoding UTF8

CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS
    core AUTHORIZATION <owner-role>;

\echo '  >> Creating Core Table(s) (optionally seed) ...'
\ir schemas/core/initialize.sql

\echo '  >> Creating other Schemas and Optionally Seed Table(s) ...'
\ir schemas/auth/initialize.sql
\ir schemas/events/initialize.sql
\ir schemas/projects/initialize.sql

COMMIT;

\echo 'Completed <Project> Initialization'
